Hepatitis E (HEV) (Fecal-Oral ‘Enteric’)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Acute jaundice/scleral icterus, Dark urine/pale stools, High mortality in third-trimester pregnancy, Positive anti-HEV IgM

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-018
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of debilitating nausea, aching abdominal pain, and rapid development of jaundice following recent travel, requiring urgent assessment to rule out severe acute hepatitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with an acute onset of severe vomiting, right upper quadrant pain, and new dark urine/pale stools after returning from a developing region. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate hydration status, rule out acute biliary obstruction, and assess for early signs of fulminant hepatic failure prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been violently sick for days, my stomach constantly aches, and my urine looks like black coffee."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 5 days ago with an acute onset of mild fever, profound generalized fatigue, and systemic malaise. This progressed rapidly to severe, debilitating anorexia, distinct nausea, and recurrent vomiting. The patient notes an aching, dull, continuous right upper quadrant abdominal pain. Over the last 24 hours, there has been a sudden development of distinct clinical jaundice, profound scleral icterus, markedly dark 'tea-coloured' urine, and extremely pale, acholic stools. The patient reports a history of recent travel to an endemic developing region (rural India) 3 weeks ago where they consumed untreated water. The patient is not pregnant.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but highly restricted by fatigue and intense nausea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of dehydration due to recurrent vomiting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg (down 2kg from baseline)
General: Visibly jaundiced, fatigued, dry mucous membranes, intermittently dry-heaving into a basin.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a soft abdomen with distinct, aching right upper quadrant tenderness. The liver edge is palpable and mildly tender. No rebound or guarding. No splenomegaly or ascites. Neurological exam reveals an alert, oriented patient with no asterixis. Intense generalized pruritus is noted with excoriations on the forearms. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Acute jaundice/scleral icterus, Dark urine/pale stools, High mortality in third-trimester pregnancy (not applicable here), and Positive anti-HEV IgM (pending serology).
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Emesis basin heavily utilized.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hepatitis E (HEV) - Acute Infection - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Hepatitis A (HAV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinically indistinguishable without serology; both are enterically transmitted and present identically, necessitating a full viral hepatitis panel to differentiate.

Acute Cholecystitis/Biliary Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a significant viral prodrome (fever, systemic malaise) prior to the pain, and the pain being dull and continuous rather than sharply colicky.

Malaria - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of cyclical high fevers/rigors, though the travel history requires a formal malaria screen.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly symptomatic icteric hepatitis following endemic travel. The severity of the vomiting and the rapid onset of jaundice necessitated an urgent on-site clinical evaluation to aggressively manage the dehydration, rule out surgical biliary pathology, and definitively confirm the absence of encephalopathy (asterixis). Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in severe intravascular volume depletion requiring hospital admission, thus satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe scheduled antiemetics (e.g., Metoclopramide or Ondansetron) to halt vomiting. Advise aggressive, small-volume oral rehydration. Urgent referral for comprehensive blood work: LFTs, U&Es, Coagulation profile, and acute viral hepatitis serology (including Anti-HEV IgM, HAV, HBV, HCV).
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the antiemetic exactly 30 minutes before attempting to drink oral rehydration solution, the patient will numb the nausea reflex; keeping the fluid down will reward the patient with a rapid decrease in the severe lethargy, reinforcing strict adherence to the medication schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP or pathology review in 24-48 hours to assess synthetic liver function (INR) and review serology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient is entirely unable to keep fluids down for over 12 hours, develops severe confusion, excessive sleepiness, easy bruising, or the abdominal pain becomes agonizing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive viral serology; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: In healthy non-pregnant adults, Hepatitis E generally follows a self-limiting, highly benign clinical course, though the fatigue and jaundice may take weeks to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your liver is currently inflamed but expected to heal completely. You must go to the emergency department immediately if you stop passing urine, if you start acting confused, or if you notice unusual bruising.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You contracted a virus from contaminated water that has temporarily swollen your liver. There is no cure for the virus itself, so our goal is strictly to keep you hydrated and comfortable while your immune system clears it.
Actionable Steps: Stop all alcohol and paracetamol immediately. Sip fluids constantly. Wash your hands thoroughly with soap after using the toilet to prevent transmission.

